WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.209 --> 00:00:07.779
All right, so this is the
state of the Shiny website

00:00:07.779 --> 00:00:09.029
at the time of this recording.

00:00:09.029 --> 00:00:12.449
So it might be different by the
time people actually see this.

00:00:12.449 --> 00:00:16.249
But if we just start from
this landing page for Shiny

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:18.829
for Python, then you
can click on Examples.

00:00:18.829 --> 00:00:20.851
And there's a whole
bunch of examples here.

00:00:20.851 --> 00:00:22.559
And you can select
them from the sidebar.

00:00:22.559 --> 00:00:23.809
So I'm just going to go down--

00:00:23.809 --> 00:00:26.779
Wordle's all the way down
here in this advanced section.

00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:29.509
And here's our Wordle app.

00:00:29.509 --> 00:00:31.069
What's your Wordle
starting word?

00:00:31.069 --> 00:00:32.459
My Wordle starter?

00:00:32.459 --> 00:00:34.904
It's arise.

00:00:34.904 --> 00:00:35.779
Can we play Wordle?

00:00:36.049 --> 00:00:37.699
I haven't played Wordle
since the last [INAUDIBLE]..

00:00:37.699 --> 00:00:39.109
Let me make this a
little bit bigger here.

00:00:39.109 --> 00:00:39.609
All right.

00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:43.789
Are you still playing
Wordle every day?

00:00:43.789 --> 00:00:45.319
I am not.

00:00:45.319 --> 00:00:47.179
I was really
hardcore for a while.

00:00:47.179 --> 00:00:49.909
And then I just stopped
and forgot one day.

00:00:49.909 --> 00:00:51.529
And then I forgot the next day.

00:00:51.529 --> 00:00:54.799
And I just haven't really done
very much of it since then.

00:00:54.799 --> 00:00:57.709
All right, so my
starter is arise.

00:00:57.709 --> 00:00:59.837
I'll just type that
on my keyboard.

00:00:59.837 --> 00:01:01.879
Did you just hit Enter
to make it do that, too?

00:01:01.879 --> 00:01:02.749
Yes, I did.

00:01:02.749 --> 00:01:05.029
So yeah, and actually,
I should point out

00:01:05.029 --> 00:01:08.989
that that keyboard
interaction, that

00:01:08.989 --> 00:01:12.799
is some custom JavaScript
that's added to this app.

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:14.936
Because normally, for
a normal web page,

00:01:14.936 --> 00:01:16.519
if you just type it
in, it's not going

00:01:16.519 --> 00:01:18.439
to send the key presses
to, necessarily,

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:19.769
where you want to go.

00:01:19.769 --> 00:01:22.849
So there's a little bit
of JavaScript, which I'll

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:24.547
walk through that does that.

00:01:24.547 --> 00:01:25.339
It's pretty simple.

00:01:25.339 --> 00:01:26.449
So what's your strategy?

00:01:26.449 --> 00:01:31.489
Do you go, I'm going to keep
A, I, S in my next guess?

00:01:31.489 --> 00:01:34.309
Or are you like, nope, I'm
going to go for full coverage.

00:01:34.309 --> 00:01:37.309
I'm going to pick something
that excludes those.

00:01:37.309 --> 00:01:40.189
Yeah, unless I
think I can get it,

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:45.499
the word, in the
next guess, I usually

00:01:45.499 --> 00:01:49.549
use another word that will
cover the rest of the vowels.

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:54.109
So my next word that I
usually use is pouty.

00:01:54.109 --> 00:01:55.069
This is amazing.

00:01:55.069 --> 00:01:56.389
You have a whole strategy.

00:01:56.389 --> 00:01:57.444
[CHUCKLES]

00:01:57.444 --> 00:01:59.569
All right, so now I know
there's no other vowels.

00:01:59.569 --> 00:02:02.519
And yeah, there's
T, A, I, and S.

00:02:02.519 --> 00:02:05.974
And so far, they've all
been in the wrong spot.

00:02:05.974 --> 00:02:07.849
And my guess would be
that the S is last.

00:02:07.849 --> 00:02:09.889
That's common.

00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:11.659
Well, that would
be, if S is last,

00:02:11.659 --> 00:02:17.009
it would almost be like
blank, A, I, T, S, which--

00:02:17.009 --> 00:02:17.509
Right.

00:02:17.509 --> 00:02:20.646
Oh, except for the T and the
I can't be in those locations.

00:02:20.646 --> 00:02:22.729
I keep coming up with
words that have the letter

00:02:22.729 --> 00:02:24.739
R, which is not helpful.

00:02:24.739 --> 00:02:25.639
Yeah.

00:02:25.639 --> 00:02:26.259
Yeah.

00:02:26.259 --> 00:02:26.759
Stain.

00:02:26.759 --> 00:02:28.279
Stain?

00:02:28.279 --> 00:02:29.839
Stain?

00:02:29.839 --> 00:02:32.329
S-T-- maybe.

00:02:32.329 --> 00:02:33.506
That's pretty good.

00:02:33.506 --> 00:02:34.089
That's good.

00:02:34.089 --> 00:02:36.249
Hey!

00:02:36.249 --> 00:02:37.049
Nice work, Jesse.

00:02:37.049 --> 00:02:38.709
[LAUGHING]

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:40.189
Teamwork.

00:02:40.189 --> 00:02:41.079
Nice.

00:02:41.079 --> 00:02:44.539
That was pretty good-- three.

00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:45.039
Awesome.

00:02:45.039 --> 00:02:46.779
And then it's got
a nice little--

00:02:46.779 --> 00:02:48.129
you've got a little pop-up.

00:02:48.339 --> 00:02:50.619
Yeah, you can take this, and
you can copy and paste it

00:02:50.619 --> 00:02:53.379
into Twitter and then
just really confuse

00:02:53.379 --> 00:02:55.659
the heck out of everyone there.

00:02:55.659 --> 00:02:59.994
[LAUGHS] That's great.

00:02:59.994 --> 00:03:02.739
Now, didn't you build this
so that you could play in

00:03:02.739 --> 00:03:04.389
as many guesses as you wanted?

00:03:04.389 --> 00:03:05.109
Yes.

00:03:05.109 --> 00:03:09.192
That was-- sometimes
I get a little lazy,

00:03:09.192 --> 00:03:11.859
and I just want to guess and not
have to think too hard about it

00:03:11.859 --> 00:03:13.129
and worry about failing.

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:13.629
So yeah.

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:14.859
So this one, it doesn't stop.

00:03:14.859 --> 00:03:17.067
You can just keep guessing
as many times as you want.

00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:21.189
I thought, let's start with
the very minimal thing,

00:03:21.189 --> 00:03:24.249
the smallest, simplest
possible thing

00:03:24.249 --> 00:03:28.159
that we can build and try to
evolve that into this game

00:03:28.159 --> 00:03:28.659
here.

00:03:28.659 --> 00:03:32.469
So that started out with just
having a simple text input

00:03:32.469 --> 00:03:35.722
and letting people type in
some stuff and hit Enter.

00:03:35.722 --> 00:03:37.389
And then from there,
it's like, OK, now,

00:03:37.389 --> 00:03:39.849
let's limit that
to five characters.

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:42.219
Let's actually add a
word list so that they're

00:03:42.219 --> 00:03:43.479
guessing a particular word.

00:03:43.479 --> 00:03:46.779
Let's save all their previous
guesses and then display them.

00:03:46.779 --> 00:03:50.019
For the Wordle app,
I could sort of

00:03:50.019 --> 00:03:52.539
see a path from this very
simple, let's start with just

00:03:52.539 --> 00:03:58.599
text input and let's try to turn
it into this full-fledged game

00:03:58.599 --> 00:03:59.689
here.

00:03:59.689 --> 00:04:01.239
And that's something
that I guess

00:04:01.239 --> 00:04:02.489
you could get from experience.

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:04.479
Like, you can see,
OK, I think it's

00:04:04.479 --> 00:04:07.419
possible to get from
point A to point B.

00:04:07.419 --> 00:04:10.999
But sometimes you can't just
start with something really,

00:04:10.999 --> 00:04:16.419
really simple and evolve it
into something that looks nice

00:04:16.419 --> 00:04:17.899
and is sophisticated like this.

00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:20.679
So sometimes you do have
to sit-- well, for me,

00:04:20.679 --> 00:04:23.934
at least-- sometimes I have to
sit down and just figure out,

00:04:23.934 --> 00:04:25.809
what are going to be
the big parts of this,

00:04:25.809 --> 00:04:27.039
and how are they
going to fit together?

00:04:27.039 --> 00:04:29.229
How much Python did you
know when you were doing

00:04:29.229 --> 00:04:31.761
this, when you started this?

00:04:31.761 --> 00:04:33.219
So when I started
the Wordle app?

00:04:33.219 --> 00:04:34.389
I think I've had--

00:04:34.389 --> 00:04:38.079
I've been programming-- spending
most of my time in Python--

00:04:38.079 --> 00:04:42.129
well, and also in JavaScript--
for just about the last year

00:04:42.129 --> 00:04:43.579
or so.

00:04:43.579 --> 00:04:47.199
But before that, I had
barely used Python at all.

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:50.439
So but I think by the time--
when I started this app,

00:04:50.439 --> 00:04:52.089
though--

00:04:52.089 --> 00:04:52.999
when did I do this?

00:04:52.999 --> 00:04:54.969
This was about four
months ago, I think.

00:04:54.969 --> 00:04:56.259
I think it was longer.

00:04:56.259 --> 00:04:56.989
Was it?

00:04:56.989 --> 00:04:59.747
I feel like this
was January/February.

00:04:59.747 --> 00:05:00.789
Oh, you might be right.

00:05:00.789 --> 00:05:02.199
Yeah, this might have been
one of the first things.

00:05:02.199 --> 00:05:03.729
Like, hey, guys,
look what I made.

00:05:03.729 --> 00:05:07.384
They were all like, what?

00:05:07.384 --> 00:05:08.859
I remember I'd just be--

00:05:08.859 --> 00:05:10.359
at least the way
that I remember it,

00:05:10.359 --> 00:05:12.039
the story that I'm
telling myself--

00:05:12.039 --> 00:05:14.439
is that you were like,
had a couple hours,

00:05:14.439 --> 00:05:15.654
thought I'd do something fun.

00:05:15.654 --> 00:05:20.149
Now we have Wordle with
Python in the browser,

00:05:20.149 --> 00:05:21.769
and you can do
unlimited guesses.

00:05:21.769 --> 00:05:22.269
Yeah.

00:05:22.269 --> 00:05:24.429
Well, I mean it helped
that there was--

00:05:24.429 --> 00:05:26.049
we already did the R one first.

00:05:26.049 --> 00:05:28.059
That's true.

00:05:28.059 --> 00:05:32.559
So just for context, how long
have you been writing R code?

00:05:32.559 --> 00:05:34.059
I'm thinking of
[INAUDIBLE],, just--

00:05:34.059 --> 00:05:34.559
Oh, yeah.

00:05:34.559 --> 00:05:37.539
[LAUGHS] Doing serious R work?

00:05:37.539 --> 00:05:40.749
Probably the last, I would
say, maybe 11 years doing that.

00:05:40.749 --> 00:05:43.511
Yeah, so that's a
lot of time to be--

00:05:43.511 --> 00:05:44.469
That's a lot of time.

00:05:44.469 --> 00:05:48.639
--working in R. So I mean,
there's a lot of nice things

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:51.039
about Python.

00:05:51.039 --> 00:05:55.449
I think it's, in terms of
just the language itself,

00:05:55.449 --> 00:05:58.929
it's designed to
be easy to learn.

00:05:58.929 --> 00:06:04.219
And it encourages sort
of a clean coding style.

00:06:04.219 --> 00:06:05.769
There are some
annoying limitations

00:06:05.769 --> 00:06:07.569
if you come from a
functional programming

00:06:07.569 --> 00:06:11.109
language like R or JavaScript.

00:06:11.109 --> 00:06:14.199
It doesn't really support
anonymous functions.

00:06:14.199 --> 00:06:18.039
It does if they're one
statement or one expression.

00:06:18.039 --> 00:06:21.796
But you can't have a multi-line
anonymous expression,

00:06:21.796 --> 00:06:24.129
which means that you end up
creating all these functions

00:06:24.129 --> 00:06:28.119
that you have to give names
to before you can pass them

00:06:28.119 --> 00:06:30.639
to another function.

00:06:30.639 --> 00:06:34.389
And there's some really weird
stuff and difficult stuff

00:06:34.389 --> 00:06:38.019
with the tooling for
Python, just not in Python

00:06:38.019 --> 00:06:40.489
itself but all the
stuff around Python,

00:06:40.489 --> 00:06:46.044
like how to install packages
and use them in a reliable way.

00:06:46.044 --> 00:06:47.169
How do you build a package?

00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:48.879
There's a zillion
ways to do things.

00:06:48.879 --> 00:06:51.399
And that's pretty difficult.

00:06:51.399 --> 00:06:54.579
When I was first looking
into becoming a data scientist,

00:06:54.579 --> 00:06:56.319
I was going to learn Python.

00:06:56.319 --> 00:06:59.836
And it took me forever
to get it installed.

00:06:59.836 --> 00:07:01.419
Sometimes I couldn't
get it installed.

00:07:01.419 --> 00:07:04.569
But then it was like
pip install, whatever.

00:07:04.569 --> 00:07:07.899
And no one told you where
to put the command pip.

00:07:07.899 --> 00:07:10.989
And so I would put
it in my browser.

00:07:10.989 --> 00:07:11.979
Yeah, yeah.

00:07:11.979 --> 00:07:13.359
[INAUDIBLE]

00:07:13.359 --> 00:07:14.889
You run Python,
and it launches

00:07:14.889 --> 00:07:17.079
Python 2 on your computer
instead of Python 3.

00:07:17.079 --> 00:07:19.429
And then none of the stuff
that you installed with pip

00:07:19.429 --> 00:07:19.929
is there.

00:07:19.929 --> 00:07:21.714
And it's like, why
isn't that working?

00:07:24.789 --> 00:07:25.739
It's a good time.

00:07:25.739 --> 00:07:26.739
That stuff is not fun.

00:07:26.739 --> 00:07:28.089
But actually, that's
one reason that I'm

00:07:28.089 --> 00:07:30.631
really excited about all this
online editing stuff that we've

00:07:30.631 --> 00:07:34.289
built. We have this editor
here where you can just

00:07:34.289 --> 00:07:38.099
get started running
Shiny, and all the stuff's

00:07:38.099 --> 00:07:39.199
already installed.

00:07:39.199 --> 00:07:42.089
You don't have to
install Python.

00:07:42.089 --> 00:07:43.961
You don't have to
install any packages.

00:07:43.961 --> 00:07:46.169
You don't even have to run
this on a normal computer.

00:07:46.169 --> 00:07:49.619
You can run this on an iPad,
where you wouldn't even

00:07:49.619 --> 00:07:52.349
be able to install
Python, normally.

00:07:52.349 --> 00:07:56.729
And you get all
this assistance now.

00:07:56.729 --> 00:08:01.119
Like, if I want to start
working in this UI here--

00:08:01.119 --> 00:08:04.779
so I can say like
ui.input_checkbox.

00:08:09.849 --> 00:08:12.612
And then it's got all
its autocompletion stuff

00:08:12.612 --> 00:08:13.404
that's [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:13.404 --> 00:08:14.659
This is pretty new, right?

00:08:14.659 --> 00:08:15.909
This is new, actually, yeah.

00:08:15.909 --> 00:08:18.069
This actually wasn't-- this
didn't exist in the last video

00:08:18.069 --> 00:08:18.939
that we recorded.

00:08:18.939 --> 00:08:23.069
This was just implemented
in the last couple of weeks.

00:08:23.069 --> 00:08:25.709
So yeah, and then you can
hover over it and there's--

00:08:25.709 --> 00:08:27.339
it's a little bit finicky--

00:08:27.339 --> 00:08:31.249
but it gives you the whole
documentation right here.

00:08:31.249 --> 00:08:33.549
So that has been super helpful.

00:08:33.549 --> 00:08:35.768
That's really helpful for
coding in the browser.

00:08:35.768 --> 00:08:37.929
And it also has that
support for that,

00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:39.429
for the Python standard library.

00:08:39.429 --> 00:08:44.949
So I can say from
os import path.

00:08:44.949 --> 00:08:46.521
And then I can
say path.basename.

00:08:49.809 --> 00:08:54.069
And then it gives you all these
hints here to write the code.

00:08:54.069 --> 00:09:00.139
So that will hopefully be a big
help for people to get started.

00:09:00.139 --> 00:09:01.659
And again, you don't
have to install

00:09:01.659 --> 00:09:03.951
all the stuff in your computer
and get that all set up.

00:09:03.951 --> 00:09:06.609
You can get that
stuff set up, like,

00:09:06.609 --> 00:09:10.539
for example, with VS Code, which
I often use for coding Python.

00:09:10.539 --> 00:09:12.219
And it's really, really nice.

00:09:12.219 --> 00:09:14.802
But that's more stuff
you have to set up, then.

00:09:14.802 --> 00:09:16.719
It's like, OK, you've
got to set up this code.

00:09:16.719 --> 00:09:19.089
You've got to install
the Python extensions.

00:09:19.089 --> 00:09:21.699
You've got to configure the
Python extensions to give you

00:09:21.699 --> 00:09:24.549
the right sort of
assistance and have it not

00:09:24.549 --> 00:09:26.059
flag too many things as errors.

00:09:26.059 --> 00:09:28.519
So there's a lot of work,
a lot of manual work, that

00:09:28.519 --> 00:09:30.769
would be involved if you set
it up on your own system.

00:09:30.769 --> 00:09:32.799
But if you don't
want to do that,

00:09:32.799 --> 00:09:35.439
you can just come right in here
and get working right away.

00:09:35.439 --> 00:09:36.999
I feel like we're cheating.

00:09:36.999 --> 00:09:38.274
I feel like--

00:09:38.274 --> 00:09:39.269
[LAUGHS]

00:09:39.269 --> 00:09:41.019
I feel like there's
a catch, like you're

00:09:41.019 --> 00:09:43.899
going to be like, you can
do all these cool things--

00:09:43.899 --> 00:09:44.979
there's no free lunch.

00:09:44.979 --> 00:09:47.104
I mean, this isn't a theorem
or anything like that.

00:09:47.104 --> 00:09:50.619
But if I want to
learn Shiny, this

00:09:50.619 --> 00:09:52.809
feels like such an
approachable way to do it.

00:09:52.809 --> 00:09:54.249
Yeah.

00:09:54.249 --> 00:09:57.781
Eventually, you'll probably,
if you're doing a lot of Shiny,

00:09:57.781 --> 00:09:59.739
writing a lot of Shiny
code, eventually, you'll

00:09:59.739 --> 00:10:03.309
probably hit some
sort of roadblocks

00:10:03.309 --> 00:10:06.669
if you just work this way.

00:10:06.669 --> 00:10:10.024
So in developing this editor,
we've tried to remove as many

00:10:10.024 --> 00:10:10.899
of those as possible.

00:10:10.899 --> 00:10:14.222
So you can save these
files to your disk.

00:10:14.222 --> 00:10:16.389
You can load them from disk,
even though this is all

00:10:16.389 --> 00:10:19.309
running in your web browser.

00:10:19.309 --> 00:10:21.649
But when it comes to
deploying your application,

00:10:21.649 --> 00:10:26.799
you're going to have to get
into the command line world

00:10:26.799 --> 00:10:30.129
and if you want to do
stuff that you can't

00:10:30.129 --> 00:10:33.069
do in this version of Shiny
that's running completely

00:10:33.069 --> 00:10:37.219
in the browser with Python
compiled to WebAssembly.

00:10:37.219 --> 00:10:39.349
So if you want to fetch
data from a database

00:10:39.349 --> 00:10:42.409
and you don't want to share
those credentials with the rest

00:10:42.409 --> 00:10:45.229
of the world, or you just want
to work on a data set where you

00:10:45.229 --> 00:10:48.529
need a really powerful computer
to do some computation,

00:10:48.529 --> 00:10:50.209
this isn't going to
really work for you.

00:10:50.209 --> 00:10:55.039
To send the code to the user's
browser for them to execute it,

00:10:55.039 --> 00:10:56.579
that might not work.

00:10:56.579 --> 00:11:00.469
So then you have to run it
the traditional way, which

00:11:00.469 --> 00:11:02.179
is with Python
running on the server.

00:11:02.179 --> 00:11:03.929
And you're going to
have to get your hands

00:11:03.929 --> 00:11:06.079
dirty with installing
Python and configuring it

00:11:06.079 --> 00:11:07.069
and all that sort
of stuff there.

00:11:07.069 --> 00:11:08.569
I'm going to say
it's not too bad.

00:11:08.569 --> 00:11:10.519
VS Code does it for
me, and it just works.

