00:18:08.849 --> 00:18:09.509
I love it.

00:18:09.689 --> 00:18:11.939
So here's another thing
that's unusual about this app

00:18:11.939 --> 00:18:15.029
that we haven't done in
any of the other examples.

00:18:15.029 --> 00:18:20.579
And it's to create, actually,
a subclass of inputs

00:18:20.579 --> 00:18:26.099
where we specify the type
of these various inputs.

00:18:26.099 --> 00:18:30.209
So this is what Shiny knows,
like hey, when somebody presses

00:18:30.209 --> 00:18:32.251
the Enter button,
what kind of value

00:18:32.251 --> 00:18:33.459
is going to come out of that?

00:18:33.459 --> 00:18:35.829
So we say class ShinyInputs.

00:18:35.829 --> 00:18:38.434
And this makes it a
subclass of inputs.

00:18:38.434 --> 00:18:39.809
And just declare
all these things

00:18:39.809 --> 00:18:42.299
here-- the Enter button
and the Back button.

00:18:42.299 --> 00:18:47.189
Those return integer values,
as does the New_game button.

00:18:47.189 --> 00:18:48.749
And so that's so
you know, hey, this

00:18:48.749 --> 00:18:52.529
doesn't return a
string or a Boolean.

00:18:52.529 --> 00:18:53.701
It returns a number.

00:18:53.701 --> 00:18:55.409
So inside of the server
app, first I just

00:18:55.409 --> 00:18:58.649
say, hey, take the input object
and cast it to ShinyInputs.

00:18:58.649 --> 00:19:00.449
Just treat it as ShinyInputs.

00:19:00.449 --> 00:19:02.489
Then I can say--

00:19:02.489 --> 00:19:09.089
oops-- I can say
@output, @render.text,

00:19:09.089 --> 00:19:12.839
and def my function here--

00:19:12.839 --> 00:19:14.349
oh, wait, no I
need to [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:14.349 --> 00:19:16.049
and call it def txt.

00:19:16.049 --> 00:19:23.159
And if I say return
input.Enter, it's

00:19:23.159 --> 00:19:27.179
going to say, hey, there's
something wrong here.

00:19:27.179 --> 00:19:29.999
This error message is
kind of long and cryptic.

00:19:29.999 --> 00:19:33.719
But the important part
is Function return type

00:19:33.719 --> 00:19:36.869
"int" is incompatible with
Available string or None.

00:19:36.869 --> 00:19:38.684
So it's complaining about this.

00:19:38.684 --> 00:19:41.249
This returns a number,
but this render text

00:19:41.249 --> 00:19:45.119
wants a function that
returns a string.

00:19:45.119 --> 00:19:48.239
What I would have to do is
convert that to a string.

00:19:48.239 --> 00:19:50.819
Now, it turns out that
if you handle the number

00:19:50.819 --> 00:19:53.502
to render text, it'll try to
convert it to a string, anyway.

00:19:53.502 --> 00:19:55.169
But there's other
weird objects that you

00:19:55.169 --> 00:19:58.379
might give to it that
have the wrong shape.

00:19:58.379 --> 00:20:02.189
Like if you return
a dictionary to it

00:20:02.189 --> 00:20:04.979
or if you return to some sort of
arbitrary weird Python object,

00:20:04.979 --> 00:20:07.799
it might not know
how to handle it.

00:20:07.799 --> 00:20:12.419
So having these-- declaring
the types of these inputs

00:20:12.419 --> 00:20:15.389
and having it check
them is really helpful.

00:20:15.389 --> 00:20:18.239
Well, if you are really strict
about things, so if I say,

00:20:18.239 --> 00:20:23.639
hey, I've got X, and
X is a dictionary of--

00:20:23.639 --> 00:20:28.139
or sorry, it's a
list of strings,

00:20:28.139 --> 00:20:32.482
I can't say, assign this Boolean
value to that list of strings--

00:20:32.482 --> 00:20:33.399
well, for two reasons.

00:20:33.399 --> 00:20:34.441
One is that it's Boolean.

00:20:34.441 --> 00:20:38.499
The other one is
that it's not a list.

00:20:38.499 --> 00:20:40.949
So it's not a list,
and it's not a string.

00:20:40.949 --> 00:20:44.549
But maybe I said, hey,
X is a list of strings.

00:20:44.549 --> 00:20:48.209
I'll assign it this
empty brackets first.

00:20:48.209 --> 00:20:50.279
And then I can say
x.append(input.hard).

00:20:53.739 --> 00:20:55.619
So it's like, hey, no.

00:20:55.619 --> 00:20:56.519
This is a Boolean.

00:20:56.519 --> 00:20:57.606
This is not a string.

00:20:57.606 --> 00:20:58.689
You've still got an error.

00:20:58.689 --> 00:21:00.209
So then I can say,
oh, you know what?

00:21:00.209 --> 00:21:00.751
That's right.

00:21:00.751 --> 00:21:03.419
I actually need to convert
this to a string first.

00:21:03.419 --> 00:21:12.149
Or maybe I want to say, hey,
yes, if it's input.hard else

00:21:12.149 --> 00:21:13.409
"no."

00:21:13.409 --> 00:21:17.339
So this expression is going
to return to either yes or no,

00:21:17.339 --> 00:21:23.159
depending on the
value of input.hard.

00:21:23.159 --> 00:21:25.319
So having these
types can help you

00:21:25.319 --> 00:21:29.159
avoid bugs in your
code in combination

00:21:29.159 --> 00:21:32.489
with a smart editor
like this that

00:21:32.489 --> 00:21:34.541
will flag these type errors.

00:21:34.541 --> 00:21:36.749
Yeah, actually, one thing
that I wanted to talk about

00:21:36.749 --> 00:21:41.859
was this use of reactive
values here, reactive.value.

00:21:41.859 --> 00:21:45.249
So there is a bunch
of these here.

00:21:45.249 --> 00:21:47.489
One of them is the target word.

00:21:47.489 --> 00:21:49.019
One of them is
all of the guesses

00:21:49.019 --> 00:21:51.389
that the user has
made in this round,

00:21:51.389 --> 00:21:56.084
whether the game has ended, and
what are the current guess--

00:21:56.084 --> 00:21:57.459
the current letters
in the guess.

00:21:57.459 --> 00:22:00.669
So if I type A, R,
I, those letters

00:22:00.669 --> 00:22:02.649
are the letters in
the current guess.

00:22:02.649 --> 00:22:04.659
You don't see this for
really basic Shiny apps.

00:22:04.659 --> 00:22:09.219
You don't see this
reactive.value stuff.

00:22:09.219 --> 00:22:11.569
Let me pull up s really
simple example here.

00:22:11.569 --> 00:22:13.939
So let's go to this
basic app here.

00:22:13.939 --> 00:22:17.889
So this basic app, you
just have an input--

00:22:17.889 --> 00:22:21.579
input.n, and then that
gets used by this output.

00:22:21.579 --> 00:22:27.629
So for an app like this, if
I set the state of an input,

00:22:27.629 --> 00:22:31.289
that will be consumed
by the output.

00:22:31.289 --> 00:22:32.279
And that's that.

00:22:32.279 --> 00:22:34.589
And so there's no
recording of history.

00:22:34.589 --> 00:22:37.619
Like what was the previous
state of an input, that just

00:22:37.619 --> 00:22:39.509
isn't part of this application.

00:22:39.509 --> 00:22:41.741
That's just totally--
it's just totally gone.

00:22:41.741 --> 00:22:43.199
And that's what
happens if you just

00:22:43.199 --> 00:22:46.229
have inputs just that are
used by outputs but nothing

00:22:46.229 --> 00:22:48.639
to record the previous states.

00:22:48.639 --> 00:22:54.309
So here, I'm using reactive
values to record the history.

00:22:54.309 --> 00:22:55.739
So the history,
in this case, was

00:22:55.739 --> 00:22:59.139
that I pressed A, R, I. And
then I can fill it out, S, E.

00:22:59.139 --> 00:23:04.289
And if I hit Enter,
then arise will

00:23:04.289 --> 00:23:08.346
be recorded in all_guesses.

00:23:08.346 --> 00:23:09.929
So when you see these
reactive values,

00:23:09.929 --> 00:23:13.259
that's often what it's
used for is to help

00:23:13.259 --> 00:23:14.789
record some previous state.

00:23:14.789 --> 00:23:18.209
And that's important for
many more sophisticated

00:23:18.209 --> 00:23:19.119
applications.

00:23:19.119 --> 00:23:22.439
All right, so another unusual
feature about this app

00:23:22.439 --> 00:23:27.329
is, so I'm creating
reactive effects that

00:23:27.329 --> 00:23:30.269
respond to each key press.

00:23:30.269 --> 00:23:31.769
But I'm doing that in a loop.

00:23:31.769 --> 00:23:34.569
So that is a little bit unusual.

00:23:34.569 --> 00:23:37.269
So let's see here.

00:23:37.269 --> 00:23:41.054
So I created a list
called Keys that's--

00:23:41.054 --> 00:23:42.839
where is it?

00:23:42.839 --> 00:23:43.479
It's up here.

00:23:43.479 --> 00:23:47.039
So this is all the letters in
the keyboard, or all the ones

00:23:47.039 --> 00:23:48.429
that we want to use.

00:23:48.429 --> 00:23:52.829
And so I had all these
action buttons here, right?

00:23:52.829 --> 00:23:57.179
This one is named Q, W, E, R.
Those are all individual action

00:23:57.179 --> 00:23:57.959
buttons.

00:23:57.959 --> 00:24:06.359
And I want this application
to update the current guess

00:24:06.359 --> 00:24:12.001
letters whenever one of
those buttons is pressed.

00:24:12.001 --> 00:24:14.459
Now, one way to do that is I
could create a reactive effect

00:24:14.459 --> 00:24:16.169
for every single one.

00:24:16.169 --> 00:24:19.092
So I could do-- oops,
let's not put it there.

00:24:19.092 --> 00:24:20.009
I'll put it down here.

00:24:20.009 --> 00:24:24.539
I could say-- sorry--

00:24:24.539 --> 00:24:34.289
reactive.Effect, reactive.event,
and this would listen

00:24:34.289 --> 00:24:35.999
to input.Q--

00:24:40.949 --> 00:24:42.419
I think it's capitalized.

00:24:42.419 --> 00:24:44.669
And then I'd say def_.

00:24:44.669 --> 00:24:48.059
And then in here, I'd say, OK,
when the Q button is pressed,

00:24:48.059 --> 00:24:53.239
take the
current_guess_letters and--

00:24:53.239 --> 00:24:57.069
or actually, this is a
little bit complicated.

00:24:57.069 --> 00:24:59.249
current_guess_letters.

00:24:59.249 --> 00:25:02.829
And go get its
function, copy it.

00:25:02.829 --> 00:25:04.929
I'll just call this x--

00:25:04.929 --> 00:25:15.539
x.append("q") and then
current_guess_letters.set(x).

00:25:15.539 --> 00:25:18.069
So there's actually a
few things going on here.

00:25:18.069 --> 00:25:20.879
This is actually an
important enough digression

00:25:20.879 --> 00:25:23.939
that I'm going to
do it right now.

00:25:23.939 --> 00:25:28.499
current_guess_letters,
it will return a list.

00:25:28.499 --> 00:25:32.039
And you see that I'm copying
this list, modifying it,

00:25:32.039 --> 00:25:35.039
and then setting
current_guess_letters

00:25:35.039 --> 00:25:37.799
to that new list, instead
of just taking that list

00:25:37.799 --> 00:25:39.279
and modifying it.

00:25:39.279 --> 00:25:43.109
So the reason for
that is because this

00:25:43.109 --> 00:25:44.619
is a reactive value.

00:25:44.619 --> 00:25:49.019
And in order for it
to inform everyone

00:25:49.019 --> 00:25:50.609
that consumes-- all
of the things that

00:25:50.609 --> 00:25:55.739
use that reactive value,
they need to know,

00:25:55.739 --> 00:25:59.579
hey, the value here has
changed in order for them

00:25:59.579 --> 00:26:03.569
to invalidate and to know
that they have to recompute.

00:26:03.569 --> 00:26:09.179
Now, if I were to just take
this and then append q to it

00:26:09.179 --> 00:26:12.839
and not do this copying
and setting stuff,

00:26:12.839 --> 00:26:16.379
then I would modify
that list, but I

00:26:16.379 --> 00:26:20.699
wouldn't be informing everyone
that it had been updated.

00:26:20.699 --> 00:26:25.859
And also, it could confuse
some other code, if I just

00:26:25.859 --> 00:26:27.159
modify that object in place.

00:26:27.159 --> 00:26:29.576
So that's actually one important
difference between Python

00:26:29.576 --> 00:26:34.809
and R is that, in R, most
objects are immutable.

00:26:34.809 --> 00:26:38.789
And so you actually can't
cause this sort of problem.

00:26:38.789 --> 00:26:42.629
But in Python, you
have to be aware

00:26:42.629 --> 00:26:46.289
of what objects can
be changed in place

00:26:46.289 --> 00:26:50.399
and how that can cause problems
with this reactive computation.

00:26:50.399 --> 00:26:54.209
Now, I don't think that Shiny
is unique in this respect.

00:26:54.209 --> 00:26:56.939
I know if you're working
with Pandas data frames,

00:26:56.939 --> 00:26:58.979
the same thing can also happen.

00:26:58.979 --> 00:27:01.259
But it is one more thing
to be aware of compared

00:27:01.259 --> 00:27:02.429
to the R version of Shiny.

00:27:02.429 --> 00:27:05.969
We would have to do
this for every single key

00:27:05.969 --> 00:27:06.899
on our keyboard.

00:27:06.899 --> 00:27:08.219
Right.

00:27:08.219 --> 00:27:10.109
So I did this for the
letter Q. I did it

00:27:10.109 --> 00:27:15.329
for the letter W and
E, R, T, all of those.

00:27:15.329 --> 00:27:17.902
But obviously,
that's really tedious

00:27:17.902 --> 00:27:19.319
and will take up
a bunch of space.

00:27:19.319 --> 00:27:21.652
And then if I found out that
there was a bug in my code,

00:27:21.652 --> 00:27:25.109
I'd have to change all of
them, which is a huge pain.

00:27:25.109 --> 00:27:32.429
So instead of doing it this way,
what I did was I created a--

00:27:32.429 --> 00:27:36.419
so there's a function
make_key_listener

00:27:36.419 --> 00:27:41.369
which creates this
reactive effect that

00:27:41.369 --> 00:27:43.979
listens for a specific key.

00:27:43.979 --> 00:27:46.059
So it can take the
name of the key.

00:27:46.059 --> 00:27:51.029
So the first one would be Q.
And it's a reactive event.

00:27:51.029 --> 00:27:53.609
It's listening for input[key].

00:27:53.609 --> 00:27:57.029
This is actually equivalent to--

00:27:57.029 --> 00:28:01.169
I can do input.Q here,
or I can do input ["Q"]..

00:28:01.169 --> 00:28:02.579
Those actually both work.

00:28:02.579 --> 00:28:05.099
Those are equivalent.

00:28:05.099 --> 00:28:07.959
The dot syntax is a
little bit more convenient

00:28:07.959 --> 00:28:09.959
if you're just typing it
out for a single thing.

00:28:09.959 --> 00:28:12.389
But if you need to
programmatically access

00:28:12.389 --> 00:28:17.439
an input value, you can do
it this way, in the bracket.

00:28:17.439 --> 00:28:19.739
So I'm saying,
listen for whatever

00:28:19.739 --> 00:28:23.549
key I was told to listen to,
and then do all that same stuff

00:28:23.549 --> 00:28:25.499
that we just saw,
except the key here

00:28:25.499 --> 00:28:31.079
is, instead of hard coding Q,
it comes in from this function

00:28:31.079 --> 00:28:32.619
call.

00:28:32.619 --> 00:28:34.949
So if I call this function,
I can pass it the letter Q

00:28:34.949 --> 00:28:38.769
and the letter W
and E and so on.

00:28:38.769 --> 00:28:40.139
And that's what I'm doing here.

00:28:40.139 --> 00:28:44.249
I have a for loop that creates
the key listener for all

00:28:44.249 --> 00:28:45.832
of those keys.

00:28:45.832 --> 00:28:47.999
And again, there's exceptions
for entering backbones

00:28:47.999 --> 00:28:49.541
because those are
handled separately.

00:28:49.541 --> 00:28:53.219
Those have different
behavior from the other keys.

00:28:53.219 --> 00:28:55.649
But essentially, it just
it calls this in a loop.

00:28:55.649 --> 00:29:00.599
Now, you might be
thinking, hey, can I just,

00:29:00.599 --> 00:29:03.329
